<!-- cm_07_ev_v2.xml
     Current Measures v2: Pakistan EV/NEV policy with ICE suppression.

     v1 boosted BEV share-weights only (4W: 1.0->7.0, 2W/3W: 2.0->8.0).
     Result: 246 PJ transport electricity by 2050 — far below MSG (652 PJ)
     and the NEV Policy targets (50-90% new sales by 2030-2040).

     v2 adds TWO levers:
       1. HIGHER BEV share-weights (4W: 2.0->15.0, 2W/3W: 3.0->18.0)
       2. NEW: ICE (Liquids) suppression — declining share-weights for ICE
          technologies to represent registration restrictions, fuel economy
          standards, import bans on ICE vehicles implied by the NEV Policy.

     In GCAM's logit competition, BEV boost alone is insufficient because
     ICE keeps competing at its natural cost advantage. The "push" (EV
     mandates/subsidies) must be paired with "pull" (ICE restrictions).

     NEV Policy 2025-2030 targets:
       2W/3W: 2.05M vehicles by 2030, 50% of new sales
       4W:    99K vehicles by 2030, 30% of new sales
       Buses: 2,238 by 2030, 50% of new sales
       Trucks: 2,996 by 2030, 30% of new sales
       Ultimate: 90% of all new vehicle sales by 2040

     Target: 300-400 PJ transport electricity by 2050

     RISK: Medium-High. If model fails:
       1. Reduce ICE suppression (raise Liquids SW from 0.2 to 0.4 at 2050)
       2. Reduce freight BEV SW first (least certain adoption)
       3. Reduce bus BEV SW second
-->
